= Register-transfer level =

{{:Register-transfer level/Simple}}

== Definition and boundary ==
'''Register-transfer level''' ('''RTL''') is an abstraction for describing digital circuits in terms of stored state, transfers between registers, and combinational transformations of signals. In a typical synchronous design, state is sampled at active clock edges; combinational logic determines candidate next-state values between edges.<ref name="ieee">IEEE, ''IEEE Std 1800-2023: SystemVerilog—Unified Hardware Design, Specification, and Verification Language'', https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/ .</ref>

RTL is a '''design representation''', not a programming language or a physical implementation. Hardware description languages such as Verilog, SystemVerilog and VHDL can express RTL, but they also support other forms of modeling and verification. An RTL source file does not itself establish that the design is synthesizable, meets timing, or works on silicon.

== State and combinational behavior ==
A register retains a value across clock cycles. Combinational logic produces outputs from current inputs and state; a clocked element captures its input according to its clock and reset behavior. A counter, for example, can update its state to the previous value plus one on each enabled rising edge, modulo its register width.

== Design and verification ==
A design workflow may elaborate RTL, simulate it against a testbench, check properties, synthesize logic into a technology-specific netlist, and perform physical implementation and timing analysis. These stages answer different questions; passing simulation alone does not prove timing closure or functional correctness under all inputs.<ref name="openlane">OpenLane 2, ''Achieving Timing Closure'', https://openlane2.readthedocs.io/en/latest/usage/timing_closure/index.html .</ref>

== Scope and limitations ==
RTL normally abstracts away transistor physics, placement, routing, analog effects, and many delay details. Clock-domain crossings, reset release, metastability, asynchronous interfaces and constraints need explicit engineering treatment beyond a simple single-clock model. Some behavioral HDL constructs are valid in simulation but cannot be synthesized into the intended hardware.

